                                            "summary":  "Santoli argues that the phase of AI investing characterized by unlimited promise and easy winners Ã¢â‚¬â€ where virtually every AI-adjacent stock rose Ã¢â‚¬â€ is definitively over. The tech sector\u0027s forward P/E multiple has contracted from 29 to 21 over the past year, a meaningful de-rating even as earnings have grown. He references the 1990s Nasdaq pattern to contextualize where the AI cycle sits: some analysts invoke that comparison to suggest it is \u0027early,\u0027 but Santoli notes the dynamic has fundamentally changed from the euphoria-driven phase. The shift predates the recent wave of concern about AI\u0027s destructive potential and the industry\u0027s calls for development restraint.",
                                            "take":  "Santoli is calling the end of the \u0027just own everything AI\u0027 phase, and the P/E compression data backs him up. This is not bearish on AI Ã¢â‚¬â€ it is bearish on undifferentiated AI exposure. The transition he is describing is from multiple expansion to earnings validation, which is actually healthy: it forces the market to price AI companies on actual economics rather than narrative. For equity allocators, the implication is to rotate from broad AI ETF exposure toward companies with demonstrated AI monetization and pricing power Ã¢â‚¬â€ the picks-and-shovels trade is maturing into a fundamentals trade.",
