                                                              "title":  "Interest Rates and Stock Prices: An Old Debate Flares up!",
                                                              "source":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                              "author":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                              "url":  "https://aswathdamodaran.blogspot.com/2026/09/interest-rates-and-stock-prices-old.html",
                                                              "date":  "2026-09-10",
                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgDslAhzkZu_Oq6rigZLlCaVn-tfCOa1vu3ybROAgHuWdrNTjeWjdAqX6_6ZHMIDNlYg6MmxdA-NciCoeOAwGWdq2GoeHsO1qvCXbrDMAMDkl9uJ5b2cR0cMQAqoH-hEAjvSIA1y6Z3kqp31L-8KS0qvM7hdLfxbLCtu17Ke766hvMmqsGR26fCLqJtnaA/w400-h285/USTreasuryRates2026.jpg",
                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran revisits the long-running debate over how rising interest rates affect equity valuations, prompted by US Treasury yields reaching 20-year highs in 2026. He shows that while corporate borrowing costs have increased due to the higher risk-free rate, equity markets have largely held up because companies have grown earnings fast enough to offset the discount rate headwind. The impact is highly uneven across sectors: energy and technology have outperformed while consumer-facing companies have been most exposed. He concludes that the relationship between rates and stock prices is far less mechanical than conventional wisdom suggests.",
                                                              "take":  "Damodaran\u0027s central insight here is that the \u0027higher rates = lower stocks\u0027 rule is a lazy heuristic, not a law. The companies absorbing the rate shock best are those with pricing power and capital-light models ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â which tracks the ongoing bifurcation between mega-cap tech and rate-sensitive consumer/real estate sectors. For investors, this is a call to stop using macro rate calls as a reason to go risk-off broadly, and instead ask company by company whether cash flows can outrun the discount rate. The debt-heavy, low-margin businesses remain genuinely vulnerable; everything else deserves a fresh look.",
                                                              "isNew":  true
