                                                              "title":  "We ain\u0027t seen nothing yet.",
                                                              "source":  "Downtown Josh Brown",
                                                              "author":  "Josh Brown",
                                                              "url":  "https://www.downtownjoshbrown.com/p/we-ain-t-seen-nothing-yet",
                                                              "date":  "2026-08-02",
                                                              "imageUrl":  null,
                                                              "summary":  "Josh Brown argues that current data center and compute capacity will prove wildly insufficient for the agentic AI era that is coming. Drawing on a conversation with Big Technology journalist Alex Kantrowitz, the post makes the case that Wall Street\u0027s fears of overbuilding are based on today\u0027s chatbot workloads ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â not on the exponentially greater demand that will come from a billion personal AI assistants, autonomous vehicles, and humanoid robots operating simultaneously. The piece frames the market\u0027s skepticism about AI capex as a failure of imagination rather than a prudent valuation concern.",
                                                              "take":  "Brown is making a structurally bullish bet on compute infrastructure by flipping the overbuilding narrative on its head: if the agentic future is even half as compute-intensive as industry insiders project, today\u0027s hyperscaler capex looks like rational pre-positioning, not reckless spending. The risk to this view is timing ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â \u0027we will need it eventually\u0027 can still produce a brutal multi-year period of excess capacity and margin compression before demand catches up. But for long-horizon investors, this piece reinforces conviction in picks-and-shovels AI infrastructure plays (NVDA, AMD, custom silicon, power/cooling) over application-layer companies whose moats are still unproven.",
                                                              "isNew":  true
