                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/fit=scale-down,quality=80,format=auto,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/eaff9351-21ae-4e4c-88d6-883dcd1d79bb/ron_baron.png?t=1784993976",
                                                              "summary":  "The piece profiles Ron Baron, whose mutual funds have been the top-performing in the US since 2003, generating over $60 billion in returns for investors. A substantial share of those gains trace directly to early, concentrated positions in Tesla (purchased from $14/share beginning in 2014) and SpaceX (a $25 billion stake). Baron\u0027s approach centers on holding high-conviction ideas through extreme volatility and public skepticism for periods measured in decades, not quarters. His track record challenges the active management industry\u0027s broader underperformance narrative by showing that a concentrated, patient approach can produce extraordinary results.",
                                                              "take":  "Baron\u0027s record is a useful corrective to the reflexive \u0027just buy index funds\u0027 consensus ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â but it also illustrates why copying the style is treacherous. His outperformance is essentially attributable to two positions that required tolerating years of brutal drawdowns and widespread ridicule. The lesson isn\u0027t \u0027buy and hold Tesla\u0027; it\u0027s that generational wealth creation in public markets requires identifying companies with 10x+ optionality early, sizing meaningfully, and having the psychological and institutional durability to ignore market noise. Very few individual investors or fund managers can sustain that discipline, which is precisely why the results are so rare and so concentrated.",
                                                              "isNew":  true
