                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran argues that the popular belief that higher interest rates automatically depress stock prices is an oversimplification that ignores how companies adapt. U.S. 10-year Treasury yields rose from 4.18% to 4.75% in 2026, yet the S\u0026P 500 and NASDAQ still posted significant gains driven by over 11% analyst-projected earnings growth for both 2026 and 2027. While he finds a statistically real but modest daily correlation (stocks drop ~0.5% on days rates rise more than 3 bps), the bigger picture is that equity resilience depends on whether rate increases stem from inflation fears or strong real growth.",
                                                                                "take":  "Damodaran is doing important work debunking a heuristic that has cost investors real money. The rate-equals-bad-for-stocks narrative is a mental shortcut that crowds ignore the earnings numerator while fixating on the discount-rate denominator. With earnings growth running above 11% and the yield curve not signaling recession, the data supports staying invested. The actionable insight: screen for companies with pricing power or low debt loads ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â they are the ones successfully adapting that Damodaran references, and that is where the alpha actually lives in a higher-rate world.",
