                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran dissects how venture capital\u0027s obsession with scaling revenue ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â regardless of unit economics ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â has structurally broken the IPO pipeline. Companies going public today are larger by revenue than those of the 1980s but far less profitable: fewer than 25% are profitable at IPO versus over 80% four decades ago. The power-law return model of VC, combined with increased public-market participation in private rounds, has removed the market-correction discipline that used to weed out non-viable business models before they reached scale.",
                                                                                "take":  "This piece should be required reading before touching any pre-profitability growth stock or late-stage private fund. The 25%-profitable-at-IPO stat is alarming ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â it means most of what comes to public markets is already a value-destruction vehicle dressed as a growth story. The practical takeaway for portfolio construction: demand a clear path to profitability, not a story about market size. Damodaran\u0027s framing of \u0027businesses that can scale vs. businesses that were just funded to scale\u0027 is the cleanest lens available for this distinction.",
